    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a really sharp way to frame it—focusing on \"attacking the math\" to puncture the emotional side of the argument. My student is definitely going to use that.\n\nBut they're worried about the \"inevitability\" part. The affirmative side is very good at pointing to pilot programs in places like Stockton or Finland where people reported feeling less stressed and more capable of finding work. The pro-side is going to argue that these aren't \"economic fantasies\" but real-world evidence that UBI provides the stability needed to actually participate in the economy.\n\nHow can the opposition counter those specific pilot studies? Is there a way to argue that those small-scale successes are actually misleading or don't translate to a national scale, or is there a deeper reason why those results shouldn't be used to justify a massive, permanent policy change?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
